(ns tutorial
  "TUTORIAL.md, to read and run in a REPL, with the models of its .lean files:
  Veil runs in this process (through the veil and lean-sys libraries), and
  jank replays what it finds."
  (:require [clojure.string :as string]
            [jank-specs.tutorial :refer [check random-run replay-array-map sh vs]]
            [veil.core :as veil]))

;;;; Modelling jank in Veil
;;
;; This file has the text of TUTORIAL.md, with forms to evaluate in a REPL. Its models are the
;; .lean files in JankSpecs/; tutorial_jank_models.jank goes through the same models written in
;; jank, as data.
;;
;; specs/ is a lein project (specs/project.clj) whose REPL runs Lean and Veil in-process, through
;; the libraries in lean-sys/ and veil/. Before starting it the first time:
;;
;;   cd lean-sys && lein install                                 # lean-sys, into ~/.m2
;;   cd veil && lein install                                     # veil, into ~/.m2
;;   cd specs && lake build +JankSpecs.Native.TinyAtom:dynlib     # Veil, built as shared libraries
;;
;; Then CIDER's jack-in from this file starts the REPL in specs/ (`lein repl` there does too).
;;
;; Evaluate the forms from this file's buffer, top to bottom: the editor sends the file's
;; namespace with each form, while (in-ns 'tutorial) typed in the REPL doesn't stick in jank 0.1.
;; The forms are in (comment ...) blocks, with their output as `;=>` notes.
;;
;; - `vs` is a Veil session: Lean, with Veil imported, in this process. The first form that uses
;;   it creates it, in a few seconds.
;; - (check "file") elaborates a Veil file in the session and prints what Veil found:
;;   counterexamples, proof tables, counterexamples to induction. (check "file" true) prints
;;   everything, whole states included. After editing a model, run (check ...) again.
;; - veil/model-check, veil/prove and veil/system, given the name of a module loaded by `check`,
;;   return Veil's results as jank data: the counterexamples' states, each proof obligation's
;;   status, and the steps a state allows.
;; - (sh "command") runs a shell command in specs/: the reproduction scripts.
;; - Other forms replay Veil's counterexamples on jank, step by step.
;;
;; These helpers are in src/jank_specs/tutorial.jank.

;;;; Veil and TLA+
;;
;; Veil's constructs and their TLA+ counterparts:
;;
;;   VARIABLES                      individual (one value), function, relation (a set of tuples)
;;   CONSTANTS                      type, and immutable fields, given per instance
;;   Init                           after_init { ... }
;;   Next == A1 \/ A2 \/ ...        one `action` each: any enabled one, with any arguments
;;   an enabling condition          require
;;   an invariant checked by TLC    safety [name] ...
;;   TLC on a finite model          #model_check { thread := Fin 3 } { ... }
;;   a TLAPS proof                  #check_invariants: SMT, for any size, checked by Lean's kernel
;;
;; The same step in both:
;;
;;   Load(t) == /\ pc[t] = "idle"                       action load (t : thread) {
;;              /\ seen' = [seen EXCEPT ![t] = value]      require pc t = idle
;;              /\ pc' = [pc EXCEPT ![t] = "loaded"]       seen t := value
;;              /\ UNCHANGED <<value, swaps>>              pc t := loaded
;;                                                      }
;;
;; In Veil, fields an action doesn't assign stay unchanged, so there's no UNCHANGED.

;;;; 1. Array map transients
;;
;; Three facts from jank 0.1's C++:
;; - a small map (up to 8 entries) is an array map: a pointer to an array of key/value slots;
;; - (transient m) copies that pointer, not the array;
;; - assoc! of a key that's already there overwrites its slot in place.
;;
;; The property comes from transient's docstring ("Returns a new, transient version of the
;; collection") and was written before looking for any bug: a persistent map always holds what it
;; should. What a map should hold isn't anywhere in jank, so the model keeps it as ghost state: a
;; field that records the intended contents, updated by each step as the docs say.
;;
;; In the model (JankSpecs/Tutorial/TinyArrayMap.lean), maps point to arrays and arrays hold the
;; entries, as in the C++:
;;
;;   relation points : obj → arr → Bool            -- map o points to array a
;;   relation slot   : arr → key → val → Bool      -- array a holds k v
;;   relation should : obj → key → val → Bool      -- ghost: what o should hold
;;
;;   action transient (p : obj) (t : obj) (a : arr) {
;;     ...
;;     points t A := points p A                    -- jank: the same array
;;     should t K V := should p K V
;;   }
;;
;;   action assocBang (t : obj) (k : key) (v : val) {
;;     require ∃ A V, points t A ∧ slot A k V      -- the key is there
;;     slot A K V := decide ((points t A ∧ K = k ∧ V = v) ∨ (¬ points t A ∧ slot A K V))
;;     should t K V := decide (K = k ∧ V = v)
;;   }
;;
;;   safety [maps_hold_what_they_should]
;;     persistentMap O → ((∃ A, points O A ∧ slot A K V) ↔ should O K V)

(comment
  ;; Model checking, without and with the fix (copyOnTransient: transient copies the array), as
  ;; the file's two #model_check commands do:
  (check "JankSpecs/Tutorial/TinyArrayMap.lean")
                                        ;=> 95: ❌ Violation: safety_failure (violates: maps_hold_what_they_should)
                                        ;     State 1 (via literal(o=0, a=2, k=0, v=1)):    map 0 is {k 1}, in array 2
                                        ;     State 2 (via transient(p=0, t=2, a=0)):       transient 2 points to array 2 too
                                        ;     State 3 (via assocBang(t=2, k=0, v=0)):       array 2 now holds {k 0}: map 0 changed
                                        ;   99: ✅ No violation (explored 715 states)        with the fix

  ;; Every field of each state (points, slot, should...):
  (check "JankSpecs/Tutorial/TinyArrayMap.lean" true)

  ;; The same model check, with its result as data. The trace's states are the model's own, with
  ;; each relation as a set of tuples:
  (def r (veil/model-check @vs "TinyArrayMap" {:instance {:obj 3 :arr 3 :key 1 :val 2}
                                               :theory {:copyOnTransient false}}))
  (:violates r)
                                        ;=> [:maps_hold_what_they_should]
  (mapv (juxt :action :args) (:trace r))
                                        ;=> [[:after_init nil] [:literal {:o 0, :a 0, :k 0, :v 0}] [:transient {:p 0, :t 1, :a 1}]
                                        ;    [:assocBang {:t 1, :k 0, :v 1}]]
  (select-keys (:state (last (:trace r))) [:points :slot :should])
                                        ;=> {:points #{[0 0] [1 0]}, :slot #{[0 [0 1]]}, :should #{[1 [0 1]] [0 [0 0]]}}
                                        ;   maps 0 and 1 point to array 0, which now holds k = 1, while map 0 should hold k = 0
  (veil/model-check @vs "TinyArrayMap" {:instance {:obj 3 :arr 3 :key 1 :val 2}
                                        :theory {:copyOnTransient true}})
                                        ;=> {:result :noViolationFound, :explored-states 715, ...}

  ;; A counterexample is a statement about the model, so it has to be checked on jank.
  ;; replay-array-map does the trace's steps on jank maps:
  (replay-array-map (:trace r))
                                        ;=> {0 {:jank {0 1}, :should {0 0}}}
                                        ;   On jank, map 0 holds {0 1}; Clojure gives {0 0}, what it should hold. It reproduces, so it's
                                        ;   a jank bug; had it not reproduced, the model would have been wrong.

  ;; The same steps, written by hand; each step can be evaluated alone:
  [(def m0 {:k 0})                      ; literal(o=0, k=0, v=0)
   (def t1 (transient m0))              ; transient(p=0, t=1)
   (assoc! t1 :k 1)                     ; assocBang(t=1, k=0, v=1)
   m0]
                                        ;=> [#'tutorial/m0 #'tutorial/t1 #object [transient_array_map ...] {:k 1}]

  ;; clojure.core uses transients, so ordinary code is affected:
  [(def m2 {:a 1 :b 2})
   (into m2 {:a 9})
   m2]
                                        ;=> [#'tutorial/m2 {:a 9, :b 2} {:a 9, :b 2}]: m2 changed

  ;; A hash map (more than 8 entries) isn't affected, because its transient copies on write:
  [(def h (zipmap (range 20) (range 20)))
   (count (persistent! (assoc! (transient h) 0 99)))
   (get h 0)]
                                        ;=> [#'tutorial/h 20 0]: h kept 0

  ;; The reproduction file, run on jank and then on Clojure:
  (println (sh "jank run repro/transient-array-map-mutates-source.cljc; clojure -M repro/transient-array-map-mutates-source.cljc")))

;; My first transient model only tracked whether each transient was still active. The bug is in
;; the storage, which that model didn't describe, so it couldn't find it. A model only finds bugs
;; in what it describes; this one describes which object points to which array. A differential
;; test against Clojure found the bug first; this model then finds it in 3 steps, and checks the
;; fix before it's written in C++.

;;;; 2. swap! on atoms
;;
;; jank's swap! reads the value, computes the new one, then does a compare-and-set: if the atom
;; changed in the meantime, it starts again. The model (JankSpecs/Tutorial/TinyAtom.lean):
;;
;;   individual value : Nat
;;   function pc : thread → pc_t                   -- idle, loaded, done
;;   function seen : thread → Nat                  -- what the thread read
;;   individual swaps : Nat                        -- ghost: swap!s that took effect
;;
;;   action load (t : thread) { require pc t = idle; seen t := value; pc t := loaded }
;;
;;   action cas (t : thread) {
;;     require pc t = loaded
;;     if value = seen t then                      -- unchanged since the read
;;       value := seen t + 1
;;       swaps := swaps + 1
;;       pc t := done
;;     else
;;       pc t := idle                              -- read again
;;   }
;;
;;   safety [no_lost_update] value = swaps

(comment
  ;; NaiveAtom.lean stores without the compare. Model checking it:
  (check "JankSpecs/Tutorial/NaiveAtom.lean")
  ;=> 53: ❌ Violation: safety_failure (violates: no_lost_update)
  ;     State 1 (via load(t=1))   State 2 (via load(t=0))   State 3 (via store(t=1))   State 4 (via store(t=0))

  ;; Every field of each state; the last state has value = 1 and swaps = 2:
  (check "JankSpecs/Tutorial/NaiveAtom.lean" true)

  ;; The same model check, with its result as data:
  (def naive (veil/model-check @vs "NaiveAtom" {:instance {:thread 2}}))
  (:violates naive)
  ;=> [:no_lost_update]
  (mapv (juxt :action :args) (:trace naive))
  ;=> [[:after_init nil] [:load {:t 0}] [:load {:t 1}] [:store {:t 0}] [:store {:t 1}]]
  (:state (last (:trace naive)))
  ;=> {:value 1, :pc {0 :done, 1 :done}, :seen {0 0, 1 0}, :swaps 2}: two increments, one lost

  ;; Each step is atomic, so the trace can be replayed in one thread. The swap! without compare,
  ;; as deref then reset!:
  (let [a (atom 0)
        seen-0 @a                  ; load(t=0)
        seen-1 @a                  ; load(t=1)
        _ (reset! a (inc seen-0))  ; store(t=0)
        _ (reset! a (inc seen-1))] ; store(t=1)
    @a)
  ;=> 1: two increments, one lost

  ;; The same steps in the same order, with compare-and-set! as in jank's swap!:
  (let [a (atom 0)
        seen-0 @a
        seen-1 @a
        ok-0 (compare-and-set! a seen-0 (inc seen-0))
        ok-1 (compare-and-set! a seen-1 (inc seen-1))]
    [ok-0 ok-1 @a])
  ;=> [true false 1]: thread 1's compare fails because the atom changed, so it reads again (the
  ;   model's `pc t := idle`) and its update isn't lost

  ;; jank's swap! from four futures, a thousand swap!s each:
  (let [a (atom 0)
        fs (doall (for [_ (range 4)] (future (dotimes [_ 1000] (swap! a inc)))))]
    (doseq [f fs] @f)
    @a)
  ;=> 4000, every time

  ;; TinyAtom.lean, with three threads (and its proofs, described below):
  (check "JankSpecs/Tutorial/TinyAtom.lean")
  (veil/model-check @vs "TinyAtom" {:instance {:thread 3}})
  ;=> {:result :noViolationFound, :explored-states 71, :termination :exploredAllReachableStates}
  )

;; veil/system gives a model's executable transition system: its initial states, the steps
;; enabled in a state, and whether the properties hold in a state. States are jank data, whether
;; they come from the model or are written in jank.

(comment
  (def sys (veil/system @vs "TinyAtom" {:instance {:thread 2}}))
  (veil/initial-states sys)
  ;=> [{:value 0, :pc {0 :idle, 1 :idle}, :seen {0 0, 1 0}, :swaps 0, :maxReturned 0, :stale false}]
  (mapv (juxt :action :args) (veil/successors sys (first (veil/initial-states sys))))
  ;=> [[:load {:t 0}] [:load {:t 1}]]

  ;; A state written in jank: both threads read 0, and thread 1's compare-and-set took effect.
  ;; Thread 0's cas fails, and it goes back to idle:
  (veil/successors sys {:value 1 :pc {0 :loaded 1 :done} :seen {0 0 1 0}
                        :swaps 1 :maxReturned 1 :stale false})
  ;=> [{:action :cas, :args {:t 0}, :state {:value 1, :pc {0 :idle, 1 :done}, ...}}]

  ;; NaiveAtom's last state, in TinyAtom: no_lost_update and max_is_swaps fail.
  (veil/properties sys {:value 1 :pc {0 :done 1 :done} :seen {0 0 1 0}
                        :swaps 2 :maxReturned 1 :stale false})
  ;=> {:no_lost_update false, :results_increase true, :max_is_swaps false}

  ;; random-run uses the model as a test oracle: it takes random enabled steps until none is
  ;; left, does each one on an atom too (deref for load, compare-and-set! for cas), and compares
  ;; the model's value with the atom's after every step.
  (random-run sys)
  ;=> {:steps [[:load 1] [:cas 1] [:load 0] [:cas 0]], :value 2, :disagreement nil}
  (count (remove #(nil? (:disagreement %)) (repeatedly 200 #(random-run sys))))
  ;=> 0
  )

;; For jank's swap! with three threads, model checking finds no violation (71 states). That covers
;; every interleaving of three threads, and no more. A proof covers any number of threads, by
;; induction over steps:
;;
;; 1. the properties hold in the initial state;
;; 2. in any state where all of them hold, any step keeps them holding.
;;
;; Then they hold in every reachable state, for any number of threads and any interleaving.
;; #check_invariants asks an SMT solver both questions, for every action, and Lean's kernel checks
;; the answers (set_option veil.smt.trust false).
;;
;; The model has a second property: each swap! returns more than every swap! before it. A ghost
;; flag `stale` is set if one returns a value no larger than the largest returned so far
;; (`maxReturned`):
;;
;;   safety [results_increase] ¬ stale

(comment
  ;; TinyAtomFirstTry.lean is the first attempt, without the invariant max_is_swaps:
  (check "JankSpecs/Tutorial/TinyAtomFirstTry.lean")
  ;=>   cas
  ;       no_lost_update ... ✅
  ;       results_increase ... ❌
  ;         Pre-state:  value = 0, swaps = 0, maxReturned = 1, stale = false
  ;         Action: cas(t=0)
  ;         Post-state: value = 1, swaps = 1, maxReturned = 1, stale = true
  ;
  ;; The same proof, as data: the failed obligation, and the solver's counterexample to induction.
  (def first-try (remove :alternative-for (veil/prove @vs "TinyAtomFirstTry")))
  (map :vc (remove #(= :proven (:status %)) first-try))
  ;=> (:cas_results_increase)
  (:counterexample (first (remove #(= :proven (:status %)) first-try)))
  ;=> {:theory {},
  ;    :pre-state {:value 0, :pc {0 :loaded}, :seen {0 0}, :swaps 0, :maxReturned 1, :stale false},
  ;    :action :cas, :args {:t 0},
  ;    :post-state {:value 1, :pc {0 :done}, :seen {0 0}, :swaps 1, :maxReturned 1, :stale true}}
  ;
  ;; no_lost_update is proved. For results_increase, the solver shows a state where the property
  ;; holds and one step breaks it. No run reaches that state: maxReturned = 1 while no swap! is
  ;; done. Step 2 of the induction considers every state where the properties hold, reachable or
  ;; not. This is why model checking passed (it only visits reachable states) while the proof
  ;; failed.
  ;;
  ;; The proof needs the fact that rules this state out. The question is why maxReturned = 1,
  ;; swaps = 0 can't happen, and the answer is that the largest value returned always equals the
  ;; number of swap!s:
  ;;
  ;;   invariant [max_is_swaps] maxReturned = swaps

  (check "JankSpecs/Tutorial/TinyAtom.lean")
  ;=> every row ✅, for load and cas, max_is_swaps included
  (veil/proved? (veil/prove @vs "TinyAtom"))
  ;=> true
  ;; A missing invariant is found this way: read the counterexample to induction, find why its
  ;; state is unreachable, and add that fact as an invariant. Deleting max_is_swaps from
  ;; TinyAtom.lean and running (check ...) again shows the failure.
  )

;; The proof is about the model: every interleaving of any number of threads doing swap! as a load
;; followed by a compare-and-set loses no update and returns increasing values. It assumes the
;; model matches the C++, where one compare-and-set is one atomic step. The full model
;; (JankSpecs/Concurrency/Atom.lean) is also checked against 5000 runs of jank.

;;;; 3. The nREPL server model
;;
;; Editors talk to jank through its nREPL server, often over several connections at once. This
;; section describes how JankSpecs/Nrepl.lean was built.
;;
;; 1. Properties. They come from nREPL's documentation and from what CIDER expects: N1 every
;;    request gets an answer; N2 every response carries its request's id and session; N3 `done`
;;    comes in its own message; N4 an eval's :out holds what it printed, and only that; N5 when no
;;    eval runs, the server's stdout is its own again; N6 *1 is the session's last value; N7 an
;;    unknown op is reported as one.
;;
;; 2. Shared state in the code. Each connection gets a thread that takes its requests one at a
;;    time. To capture an eval's output, capture.jank saves file descriptor 1 (dup), flushes C's
;;    stdout buffer, points descriptor 1 at a temp file (dup2), evaluates, flushes, points
;;    descriptor 1 back, reads and deletes the temp file. Descriptor 1 and C's stdout buffer
;;    belong to the process, not the connection, so every operation on them is a step of its own:
;;    another connection's steps can come in between.
;;
;; 3. Actions, one per operation on shared state:
;;
;;    queued -take-> taken -redirect-> capturing -evaluate-> evaluated -endFlush-> flushed -release-> released -respond-> answered
;;           dup         fflush, dup2           prints go to          fflush           dup2 back,
;;                                              the buffer                             delete temp
;;
;; 4. State. The state has only what the properties need: where descriptor 1 points, each eval's
;;    backup, deleted temp files, what is in C's buffer (a flush moves it to wherever descriptor 1
;;    points at that moment), and, as ghost state, where each print ended up. The requests (which
;;    connection, what kind, which session) are constants: two connections, four requests.
;;
;; 5. Properties in Veil:
;;
;;    safety [n4_output_isolation]
;;      focus = 4 → ((printedIn X R → X = R) ∧ ¬ printedLost X ∧ ¬ printedTerminal X)
;;    safety [n5_stdout_restored] focus = 5 → ((∀ C, ¬ busy C) → ¬ stdoutTo Q)
;;
;;    `focus` selects which expected-to-fail property a run checks; see "Conventions" in
;;    AGENTS.md.

(comment
  ;; 6. Counterexamples. This elaborates the whole model, with one instance per property, in
  ;; about 30 seconds:
  (check "JankSpecs/Nrepl.lean")
  ;; N4 (two connections, r0 and r2, each printing):
  ;;   take(r0) take(r2) redirect(r0) redirect(r2) evaluate(r0) endFlush(r0)
  ;;   the flush writes r0's print into r2's temp file, so r0's output is in r2's response
  ;; N5:
  ;;   take(r0) redirect(r0)     r0's backup: the real stdout; fd 1 -> r0's temp
  ;;   take(r2)                  r2's backup: r0's temp file (saved while r0 captures)
  ;;   redirect(r2) ...
  ;;   release(r0)               fd 1 -> the real stdout; r0's temp deleted
  ;;   release(r2)               fd 1 -> r0's temp file, which is deleted, so stdout is lost

  ;; N4's counterexample as data. The theory is the file's: which connection sends each
  ;; request, what kind it is, its session, focus = 4, and no proposed fix (`fixed`).
  (def n4 (veil/model-check @vs "Nrepl"
                            {:instance {:conn 2 :req 4 :session 2}
                             :theory {:reqConn "fun r => if r.val < 2 then 0 else 1"
                                      :reqKind "fun _ => kind_t_IndT.evalPrint"
                                      :reqSession "fun _ => 0"
                                      :focus 4
                                      :fixed "fun _ => false"}}))
  [(:violates n4) (mapv (juxt :action :args) (rest (:trace n4)))]
  ;=> [[:n4_output_isolation] [[:take {:r 0}] [:take {:r 2}] [:redirect {:r 0}] ...]]

  ;; The reproduction on jank needs two connections to a fresh server (doing it to this REPL's
  ;; server would break its output), so it's a script: 20 rounds of two concurrent evals, then a
  ;; print from a future.
  (println (sh "python3 repro/nrepl-concurrent-output-swap.py"))
  ;=> expected 0, rounds where an eval's :out wasn't exactly its own print, of 20: 20
  ;   expected visible, a future's print after the rounds, on the server's stdout: lost
  ;   (or visible: whether stdout is lost depends on how the captures interleaved)

  ;; Reproductions of the other nREPL findings:
  (println (sh "python3 repro/nrepl-bad-argument-ends-connection.py"))   ; N1
  (println (sh "python3 repro/nrepl-done-with-eval-error.py"))           ; N3
  (println (sh "python3 repro/nrepl-sessions-share-star1.py"))           ; N6
  )

;; 7. Conformance testing. The harness ran scripts of four requests on a real server, many times,
;;    and checked that every outcome jank showed is one the model allows. At first it wasn't: jank
;;    showed a print on the server's own stdout with stdout working afterwards, which my first
;;    model said was impossible. That model captured output in one atomic step and sent a print
;;    to wherever descriptor 1 pointed when it was printed. Working out how that print could reach
;;    the real stdout gave two facts the first model had wrong: println writes to C's buffer, and
;;    the bytes reach descriptor 1 at the next fflush, done by whichever capture comes next; and
;;    starting a capture is three system calls, with room for another connection's steps in
;;    between. The model then got the buffer, the flush and the split between take and redirect,
;;    and every outcome conformed (24 scripts, 3 runs each).

(comment
  ;; The conformance check (src/jank_specs/conformance/nrepl.jank): it starts a server per run,
  ;; and calls the outcomes of NreplOutcomes.lean, loaded in this session, on what jank showed.
  ;; 24 scripts, 3 runs each: about 10 minutes.
  (require '[jank-specs.conformance :as conformance])
  (def nrepl-check (conformance/check "nrepl" {:random 20 :seed 3 :repeat 3}))
  (:ok? nrepl-check)
  ;=> true
  )

;; 8. Proofs. The checker found no violation of "one eval at a time per connection" in the
;;    instance. The proof for any number of connections needed the reason a connection can't run
;;    two evals at once: take requires the connection not to be busy, and it stays busy until
;;    respond.
;;
;;      invariant [eval_busy] inEval R → busy (reqConn R)
;;
;;    With it (and the invariant that only evals are captured, for N7), one_eval_per_connection,
;;    in_order, N2 and N7 are proved. Their table is pinned in the file with #guard_msgs (the
;;    build fails if a proof breaks), so `check` doesn't print it; veil/prove returns it. The 7
;;    disproven obligations are those of the properties expected to fail, which hold only for
;;    the focus that selects them (see "Conventions" in AGENTS.md); the `..._fixed_...` ones are
;;    the fixes of part 9:

(comment
  (def nrepl-proof (remove :alternative-for (veil/prove @vs "Nrepl")))
  (frequencies (map :status nrepl-proof))
  ;=> {:proven 161, :disproven 7}
  (sort (distinct (map :property (filter #(= :disproven (:status %)) nrepl-proof))))
  ;=> (:n1_connection_survives :n3_done_separate :n4_output_isolation :n5_stdout_restored
  ;    :n6_sessions_isolated)
  )

;; 9. Results. The model found five bugs, each with a counterexample and a reproduction on jank:
;;    N1, N3, N4, N5, N6. It also shows where a fix has to go: while captures swap the process's
;;    descriptor 1, concurrent evals interfere. A capture that doesn't touch it (each eval's
;;    output to a writer of its own) removes N4 and N5. The file models it, as it does a fix of
;;    each of the others: the theory value `fixed` turns them on (`fix_t`: n1, n3, n4n5, n6), and
;;    each `n…_fixed_…` property above is proved for any number of connections. N4's
;;    counterexample, with and without the fix:

(comment
  (def n4-theory {:reqConn "fun r => if r.val < 2 then 0 else 1"
                  :reqKind "fun _ => kind_t_IndT.evalPrint"
                  :reqSession "fun _ => 0"
                  :focus 4})
  (:violates (veil/model-check @vs "Nrepl" {:instance {:conn 2 :req 4 :session 2}
                                            :theory (assoc n4-theory :fixed "fun _ => false")}))
  ;=> [:n4_output_isolation]
  (select-keys (veil/model-check @vs "Nrepl" {:instance {:conn 2 :req 4 :session 2}
                                              :theory (assoc n4-theory
                                                             :fixed "fun f => f = fix_t_IndT.n4n5")})
               [:result :explored-states])
  ;=> {:result :noViolationFound, :explored-states 269}

  ;; The conformance check against the fixed model fails on jank 0.1, until the fix is written:
  (require '[jank-specs.conformance :as conformance])
  (:ok? (conformance/check "nrepl" {:random 0 :repeat 1 :fixes [:n1 :n3 :n4n5 :n6]}))
  ;=> false
  )

;;;; Summary of the method
;;
;; 1. Write the properties from the docs, before reading the code for bugs.
;; 2. Model each step of the code that touches shared state as an action (a read, a
;;    compare-and-set, a lock), and model the data layout where bugs can be.
;; 3. Add ghost state for what the properties need (swaps, should).
;; 4. Model check small instances. Reproduce each counterexample on jank; if it doesn't reproduce,
;;    fix the model.
;; 5. Use #check_invariants to prove the properties that model checking didn't violate. For each
;;    ❌, read the counterexample to induction, find why its state is unreachable, and add that as
;;    an invariant.
;; 6. Test the model against real runs of jank.
